  {"_comment": "── A BACKSLASH-NEWLINE IS NOT A COMMAND BOUNDARY, AND AN eval BODY IS CODE. Judgment Day of guard-gate-relative-paths, 2026-09-30: `find <repo> \\<newline> -delete` and `eval \"rm -rf <repo>/x\"` were ALLOWED by every driver. bash deletes an unescaped backslash-newline before it splits words, so the scan's `find ... -delete` never met the newline that stopped it; and the masker blanked eval's quoted words as data although eval runs them. An EVEN run of backslashes before the newline escapes itself, and the newline stays a real boundary."},
  {"id": "find-continuation-delete", "command": "find {{repo}} \\\n  -name a -delete", "cwd": "{{outside}}", "expect": "deny", "category": "mutator", "trigger": "names"},
